{"about":"be248e4805a64ff3ba6394f7d33da88ef38fcbfe47a3829fdf587463136ac1c3","body":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms because corroboration reports a theory's past performance in severe tests, not its future probability. Choosing the best-tested theory is a rational, non-inductive decision based on critical scrutiny; it accepts that the theory may fail tomorrow but acknowledges it is currently the most reliable guide available. This survives by accepting the cost that we possess no logical guarantee of future success, only a record of surviving falsification attempts.","kind":"mini.commitment-proposal.v1","named":"The Defensibility of Corroboration Without Induction"}